% ============================================================================
% animal-survival-adaptation — a named animal and the ONE survival strategy
% it is known for (adj-facts-stdlib, BIOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% Animals survive their environments through adaptations — traits or
% behaviors that help them navigate, stay warm, or fend off predators.
% `animal_survival_adaptation(animal, adaptation)` names one specific
% animal and the ONE adaptation it is known for. Recall is a binding
% query:
%
%     ? animal_survival_adaptation(bats, $A)   % echolocation
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `animal_survival_adaptation(animal, adaptation)`, so the lookup above is
% the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the adaptation WITH
% its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on an animal
% not one of the three the cited pages name. The query also runs BACKWARD —
% bind an adaptation and recall the animal known for it:
%
%     ? animal_survival_adaptation($X, insulation)   % polar_bear
%
% DESIGN NOTE — why this table uses a DIFFERENT predicate name from the
% already-shipped `animal_adaptation` (`animal-adaptation.adj`): that table
% already closed out arctic_fox/groundhog/canada_goose as its three rows;
% this is a genuinely different set of animals and adaptations, so it gets
% its own predicate rather than extending a closed table, mirroring how
% `monarch_life_stage` and `frog_life_stage` used distinct predicate names
% for the same shape applied to different organisms.
%
% Each row's animal is a DIFFERENT species and each names a DIFFERENT kind
% of adaptation (an echolocating sense, a physical insulating trait, and a
% visual warning display), so the table's own citation is the
% PRIMARY/first-listed source below (mirroring `animal-adaptation.adj`'s
% established multi-source discipline — an ADJ `table` carries only ONE
% table-level `source`/`locator`/`trust` block), and each OTHER row's own
% distinct citation is documented here in prose:
%
%     animal             adaptation           source page                                                        quote
%     ------------------  -------------------  ------------------------------------------------------------------  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     bats                echolocation         nationalgeographic.com/animals/mammals/facts/bats                   "To navigate dark caves and hunt after dark, microbats rely on echolocation, a system that allows them to locate objects using sound waves."
%     polar_bear          insulation           nationalgeographic.com/animals/mammals/facts/polar-bear             "Polar bears live in one of the planet's coldest environments and depend on a thick coat of insulated fur, which covers a warming layer of fat."
%     poison_dart_frog    warning_coloration   nationalgeographic.com/animals/amphibians/facts/poison-dart-frogs-1 "Their elaborate designs and hues are deliberately ostentatious to ward off potential predators, a tactic called aposematic coloration."
%
% All three citations WebFetch-verified before writing. `trust consensus`
% for all three — nationalgeographic.com's main animal-facts pages are a
% reputable, widely-cited science publisher (not a primary .gov source),
% the same tier this stdlib already reserves for other National Geographic
% sources (`animal-adaptation.adj`, `animal-habitat.adj`,
% `rainforest-layer.adj`). Note the `polar_bear` atom also appears in
% `animal-habitat.adj` for a DIFFERENT fact (its habitat, not this
% adaptation) — not a conflict, since that is a different predicate. An
% animal not one of these three — a chameleon, for example, real and
% well-known for its own adaptations, but not one this specific set of
% cited pages names here — has no row, so a recall on it ABSTAINS rather
% than inventing an adaptation. (See ../README.md;
% feedback_nothing_human_authored, feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table animal_survival_adaptation {
    columns animal, adaptation

    row (bats, echolocation)
    row (polar_bear, insulation)
    row (poison_dart_frog, warning_coloration)

    source "To navigate dark caves and hunt after dark, microbats rely on echolocation, a system that allows them to locate objects using sound waves."
    locator "https://www.nationalgeographic.com/animals/mammals/facts/bats"
    trust consensus
}
